    "answer": {
      "short": "For $b>0$, $a\\ge0$ and $a\\ne b$, $x=\\dfrac{\\sqrt{ab}}{\\sqrt a\\pm\\sqrt b}$.\n\nIf $a=b>0$, $x=\\dfrac{\\sqrt b}{2}$. If $b>0$ and $a<0$, there are no real roots. For $b\\le0$, the original real equation is undefined.\n\nApply the quadratic formula and factor $a-b$; treat the linear case separately.",
      "worked": "The original real equation requires $b>0$. Multiplying by $\\sqrt b$ gives\n$$\\begin{align*}(a-b)x^2-2a\\sqrt b\\,x+ab&=0.\\end{align*}$$\nFor $a\\ne b$, its discriminant is\n$$\\begin{align*}\\Delta&=4a^2b-4ab(a-b)\\\\\\Delta&=4ab^2.\\end{align*}$$\nIf $a<0$, there are no real roots. If $a\\geq0$,\n$$\\begin{align*}x&=\\frac{2a\\sqrt b\\pm2b\\sqrt a}{2(a-b)}\\\\x&=\\frac{\\sqrt{ab}(\\sqrt a\\pm\\sqrt b)}{(\\sqrt a-\\sqrt b)(\\sqrt a+\\sqrt b)}\\\\x&=\\frac{\\sqrt{ab}}{\\sqrt a\\mp\\sqrt b}.\\end{align*}$$\nThe two signs give the stated pair of roots; when $a=0$, both give $x=0$.\n\nIf $a=b>0$, the equation is linear:\n$$\\begin{align*}-2bx+b\\sqrt b&=0\\\\x&=\\frac{\\sqrt b}{2}.\\end{align*}$$\nFor $b\\leq0$, the original real equation is undefined.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
